               B and Kearney, Michael R",
  journal   = "Science",
  publisher = "American Association for the Advancement of Science (AAAS)",
  volume    =  387,
  number    =  6731,
  pages     = "303--309",
  abstract  = "Climate warming can induce a cost-of-living “squeeze” in
               ectotherms by increasing energetic expenditures while reducing
               foraging gains. We used biophysical models (validated by 2685
               field observations) to test this hypothesis for 10 ecologically
               diverse lizards in African and Australian deserts. Historical
               warming (1950–2020) has been more intense in Africa than in
               Australia, translating to an energetic squeeze for African
               diurnal species. Although no net impact on Australian diurnal
               species was observed, warming generated an energetic “relief” (by
               increasing foraging time) for nocturnal species. Future warming
               impacts will be more severe in Africa than in Australia,
               requiring increased rates of food intake (+10\% per hour active
               for diurnal species). The effects of climate warming on desert
               lizard energy budgets will thus be species-specific but
               potentially predictable.",
