               reveals the substantial potential of SES models to address issues
               that are of utmost importance for managing complex
               human‐environment relationships, such as: (i) the implications of
               ecological and social structure for resource management, (ii)
               uncertainty in natural and social systems and ways to address it,
               (iii) the role of coevolutionary processes in the dynamics of
               SESs, and (iv) the implications of microscale human decision
               making for sustainable resource management and conservation. The
               complexity of SESs and the lack of a common analytical framework,
