  volume    =  121,
  number    =  43,
  pages     = "e2408583121",
  abstract  = "Increasing heatwaves are threatening forest ecosystems globally.
               Leaf thermal regulation and tolerance are important for plant
               survival during heatwaves, though the interaction between these
               processes and water availability is unclear. Genotypes of the
               widely distributed foundation tree species Populus fremontii were
               studied in a controlled common garden during a record summer
               heatwave-where air temperature exceeded 48 °C. When water was not
               limiting, all genotypes cooled leaves 2 to 5 °C below air
